# kb-consolidate-boot.service — boot-path GUARD: the day's backup runs before the
# catch-up drain does.
#
# The boot catch-up (kb-consolidate-boot.timer, OnBootSec=10min) can start a
# 4.5-7h drain. A boot between 00:00 and 00:30 fires that catch-up BEFORE the
# 00:30 backup window; the drain would then run heavy extractor I/O across the
# backup and — worse on this grid — could push the day's only backup late. So the
# timer now triggers THIS guard instead of kb-consolidate.service directly.
#
# ORDERING (the systemd-idiomatic "run B, then me, then C"):
#   Wants=/After=kb-backup-local.service  — ensure a LOCAL backup has RUN, and wait for it.
#   Wants=/Before=kb-consolidate.service  — then let the catch-up drain proceed.
# Wants (not a bare After=) is required because at OnBootSec the backup is not yet
# in any transaction to order against: in the 00:15-boot case its 00:30 timer has
# not fired, and after a daytime power cut its Persistent catch-up has already
# FINISHED and gone inactive. Pulling it in guarantees the ordering in every case.
#
# WHY THE LOCAL UNIT (review #8 H10a, 2026-09-03). This guard used to pull
# kb-backup.service, whose run includes the encrypted offsite push when
# KB_OFFSITE_ENABLED=true. "One extra pg_dump per boot — cheap" was only true
# WITHOUT that leg: on 2026-09-01 the boot catch-up waited 2h59m behind the
# offsite push on modem WAN. kb-backup-local.service runs `backup.sh
# --local-only` (dump + raw archive + integrity gate + sidecar + prune, no push);
# the nightly kb-backup.timer keeps the offsite leg. The guard must never Want,
# nor order After, a unit that pushes offsite (tests/test_backup_local.py) — an
# After= alone would still park the boot path behind a Persistent catch-up's
# push. If both backup units run at one boot, backup.sh serialises the local
# legs under a lock it releases before the push, so the dumps never race.
#
# The drain itself still runs as kb-consolidate.service (its readiness wait,
# tiktoken seeding, OnFailure and post-run backup all apply unchanged); this unit
# is a pure ordering barrier and does no work of its own. It has no [Install]:
# only kb-consolidate-boot.timer starts it, never a boot target directly.
[Unit]
Description=Knowledge Vault boot catch-up guard (local backup before the catch-up drain)
Wants=kb-backup-local.service
After=kb-backup-local.service
Wants=kb-consolidate.service
Before=kb-consolidate.service
OnFailure=kb-notify-fail@%n.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
ExecStart=/bin/true
NoNewPrivileges=true
